1
00:00:00,140 --> 00:00:03,540
December 5th, 1945, Fort Lauderdale,

2
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,040
Florida. Lieutenant Charles Taylor climbs into a torpedo bomber for a routine training

3
00:00:09,040 --> 00:00:11,500
flight. He has led dozens like it before.

4
00:00:12,940 --> 00:00:14,860
Four more Avengers fall in behind him,

5
00:00:15,220 --> 00:00:19,040
14 men in total, on a mission called Navigation Problem Number One.

6
00:00:20,440 --> 00:00:24,220
Within two hours, Taylor radios that both his compasses have failed.

7
00:00:24,780 --> 00:00:27,020
He believes he is somewhere over the Florida Keys.

8
00:00:27,680 --> 00:00:30,740
He is not. By 7 that evening,

9
00:00:31,180 --> 00:00:35,140
5 aircraft and 14 men have vanished from the sky over the open Atlantic.

10
00:00:35,840 --> 00:00:39,560
The plane sent to search for them explodes in mid -air and vanishes too,

11
00:00:39,860 --> 00:00:41,300
watched by two ships at once.

12
00:00:42,740 --> 00:00:45,900
This is the story of the mystery that got built on top of that night.

13
00:00:46,360 --> 00:00:51,560
And the real, sourced, deeply unglamorous explanation for almost every piece

14
00:00:51,560 --> 00:00:54,320
of it. You're watching Mind Wired.

15
00:00:55,440 --> 00:00:58,080
This is the full story of the Bermuda Triangle.

16
00:00:58,620 --> 00:00:59,780
How the name was invented,

17
00:01:00,100 --> 00:01:04,020
what actually happened to every ship and plane usually listed inside it,

18
00:01:04,080 --> 00:01:07,100
why a librarian spent years taking the legend apart,

19
00:01:07,220 --> 00:01:11,620
case by case, and why the mystery does not survive contact with the primary record.

20
00:01:13,100 --> 00:01:15,820
Chapter One. The name nobody officially drew.

21
00:01:17,240 --> 00:01:23,960
September 1950. A wire reporter named Edward Van Winkle -Jones writes a piece for

22
00:01:23,960 --> 00:01:27,540
the 135 people lost near Florida and Bermuda over the years.

23
00:01:27,860 --> 00:01:29,020
He never calls it a triangle.

24
00:01:29,420 --> 00:01:30,920
He never claims anything paranormal.

25
00:01:31,480 --> 00:01:35,140
It reads like an ordinary story about how dangerous the ocean has always been.

26
00:01:36,640 --> 00:01:39,320
1952. A writer named George Sand,

27
00:01:39,640 --> 00:01:40,760
in a magazine called Fate,

28
00:01:41,120 --> 00:01:44,680
describes those same losses inside a rough triangle for the first time.

29
00:01:45,080 --> 00:01:47,680
He lists Flight 19, a lost freighter,

30
00:01:47,940 --> 00:01:50,620
and two British airliners that vanished a year apart.

31
00:01:50,620 --> 00:01:53,980
The shape exists now. The name still doesn't.

32
00:01:55,420 --> 00:02:00,580
February 1964. A writer named Vincent Gaddis publishes an Argosy magazine cover

33
00:02:00,580 --> 00:02:03,320
story and gives the shape its name for the first time,

34
00:02:03,580 --> 00:02:08,400
the Bermuda Triangle. He draws it from Florida to Bermuda to Puerto Rico and back through

35
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:11,240
the Bahamas and calls the losses beyond the laws of chance.

36
00:02:12,780 --> 00:02:18,120
What Gaddis never does in that entire article is compare his list of losses to

37
00:02:18,120 --> 00:02:20,420
how much traffic actually crosses that water.

38
00:02:20,920 --> 00:02:23,020
No count of ships. No count of flights.

39
00:02:23,580 --> 00:02:25,200
Just a list of the ones that went wrong.

40
00:02:25,700 --> 00:02:29,720
Without a denominator, there is no way to know if the numerator is unusual at all.

41
00:02:31,180 --> 00:02:33,260
Two years earlier, in 1962,

42
00:02:33,820 --> 00:02:37,860
a different writer had already built the emotional core of this legend out of nothing.

43
00:02:38,540 --> 00:02:43,340
Alan Eckert wrote a magazine account of Flight 19 that invented dramatic radio dialogue,

44
00:02:43,800 --> 00:02:45,320
pilots saying the ocean looked wrong,

45
00:02:45,440 --> 00:02:46,800
that nothing seemed familiar.

46
00:02:47,300 --> 00:02:49,660
None of it is in a single navy transcript.

47
00:02:50,360 --> 00:02:52,840
Gaddis' own sources leaned on that invented version.

48
00:02:54,340 --> 00:02:58,660
1965. Gaddis expands the magazine piece into a full book,

49
00:02:59,020 --> 00:03:02,620
Invisible Horizons, with a chapter called The Triangle of Death,

50
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:08,400
1974. Charles Berlitz publishes the Bermuda Triangle and it becomes a genuine

51
00:03:08,400 --> 00:03:10,780
phenomenon, reportedly selling in the millions.

52
00:03:11,360 --> 00:03:15,660
Berlitz's central twist is that the disappearances connect to the lost city of Atlantis.

53
00:03:15,660 --> 00:03:18,300
There is zero physical evidence for that claim.

54
00:03:19,020 --> 00:03:21,500
None. It is his theory and only his.

55
00:03:22,740 --> 00:03:25,700
And here is the detail every single retelling leaves out.

56
00:03:26,220 --> 00:03:28,720
Nobody has ever agreed on where the triangle actually is.

57
00:03:29,400 --> 00:03:30,840
Some versions stop at Puerto Rico.

58
00:03:31,300 --> 00:03:32,880
Some stretch into the Gulf of Mexico.

59
00:03:33,480 --> 00:03:35,540
One version stretches all the way to the Azores.

60
00:03:36,480 --> 00:03:40,880
Estimates of its total size range from about half a million square miles to nearly three

61
00:03:40,880 --> 00:03:43,480
times that. That is not a place with a border.

62
00:03:43,900 --> 00:03:47,640
That is a shape redrawn by whoever happens to be telling the story that year.

63
00:03:49,160 --> 00:03:53,880
Chapter two. The flight nobody could explain and the line that was never actually said.

64
00:03:55,320 --> 00:03:56,560
Charles Taylor was not a rookie.

65
00:03:57,460 --> 00:04:02,000
2509 flight hours. A combat tour flying torpedo bombers off a carrier in the Pacific.

66
00:04:02,520 --> 00:04:04,360
And for most of the year before that afternoon,

67
00:04:04,580 --> 00:04:08,280
he had been based in the real Florida Keys flying that exact water.

68
00:04:09,500 --> 00:04:12,220
That is what makes the next part so devastating.

69
00:04:12,780 --> 00:04:17,500
Navigation problem number one sent the five avengers out over the water around a shoal

70
00:04:17,500 --> 00:04:19,440
for a practice bombing run and back.

71
00:04:20,140 --> 00:04:24,280
Routine. Taylor had flown it or something like it many times before.

72
00:04:25,600 --> 00:04:29,220
By mid -afternoon, Taylor radios that he is uncertain of his position.

73
00:04:29,660 --> 00:04:32,600
Another instructor makes contact and asks what his trouble is.

74
00:04:33,040 --> 00:04:36,960
Taylor answers that both his compasses are out and that he is sure he is over the Keys.

75
00:04:37,220 --> 00:04:40,600
He just doesn't know how far down or how to get back to Fort Lauderdale.

76
00:04:42,260 --> 00:04:43,480
He was not over the Keys.

77
00:04:44,060 --> 00:04:48,340
A radio fix taken not long after placed the flight within a hundred miles of a point

78
00:04:48,340 --> 00:04:53,440
well north of the Bahamas out over the open Atlantic flying in the wrong direction

79
00:04:53,440 --> 00:04:56,540
entirely away from the mainland he was trying to reach.

80
00:04:57,980 --> 00:05:01,300
You may have heard a different stranger version of that radio call.

81
00:05:01,920 --> 00:05:03,260
Pilots saying the ocean looked wrong.

82
00:05:03,640 --> 00:05:07,280
Saying the aircraft closing in on them did not look like Navy planes at all like they

83
00:05:07,280 --> 00:05:08,600
were from somewhere else entirely.

84
00:05:09,120 --> 00:05:14,360
That line was invented three years earlier in that 1962 magazine story and

85
00:05:14,360 --> 00:05:17,020
it was printed as fact in Berlitz's best -selling book.

86
00:05:17,460 --> 00:05:21,800
It does not exist in a single Navy transcript from that day or any other.

87
00:05:23,260 --> 00:05:27,300
Taylor's actual final transmission was much less strange and much sadder.

88
00:05:27,400 --> 00:05:28,900
All planes close up tight.

89
00:05:29,280 --> 00:05:31,600
We will have to ditch unless we make landfall.

90
00:05:31,600 --> 00:05:35,860
When the first plane drops below 10 gallons we all go down together.

91
00:05:37,340 --> 00:05:41,500
A rescue sea plane with 13 more men aboard took off that same night to search for them.

92
00:05:41,780 --> 00:05:44,400
It sent one routine radio check and then nothing.

93
00:05:44,860 --> 00:05:49,640
At 9 .15 a tanker crew independently reported watching flames leap a hundred feet into

94
00:05:49,640 --> 00:05:54,720
the air and burn for 10 minutes on the water at almost the exact position and moment

95
00:05:54,720 --> 00:05:57,900
an escort carriers radar lost an aircraft it had been tracking.

96
00:05:58,780 --> 00:06:02,980
The accepted mundane cause is a fuel vapor explosion.

97
00:06:03,720 --> 00:06:07,600
That type of rescue sea plane had a documented history of exactly this failure.

98
00:06:08,140 --> 00:06:09,980
Fuel leaking into an enclosed bomb bay.

99
00:06:10,480 --> 00:06:12,740
27 men were gone before nine that night.

100
00:06:13,060 --> 00:06:14,720
No survivors from either aircraft.

101
00:06:15,080 --> 00:06:18,140
No wreckage from either aircraft ever recovered.

102
00:06:19,460 --> 00:06:23,880
The Navy's first report blamed Taylor outright saying he suffered something close to

103
00:06:23,880 --> 00:06:27,160
mental confusion. His mother fought that finding for two years.

104
00:06:27,600 --> 00:06:31,380
In 1947 the Navy formally amended its own record.

105
00:06:31,940 --> 00:06:34,940
The official cause became simply unknown.

106
00:06:35,500 --> 00:06:38,240
That is not a confession that anything paranormal happened.

107
00:06:38,620 --> 00:06:42,860
It means no wreckage was ever recovered to prove the navigational error theory beyond

108
00:06:42,860 --> 00:06:47,260
doubt even though it remains by far the most likely explanation.

109
00:06:48,220 --> 00:06:52,540
Chapter three. The ship that carried 306 men into nothing.

110
00:06:54,100 --> 00:06:58,920
March 1918. The Navy Collier USS Cyclops stops in Barbados for repairs.

111
00:06:59,460 --> 00:07:01,660
Loads over 10 ,000 tons of manganese ore.

112
00:07:01,980 --> 00:07:04,580
More than her rated capacity and leaves for Baltimore.

113
00:07:05,100 --> 00:07:06,200
She has never seen again.

114
00:07:06,940 --> 00:07:10,320
306 people are aboard including the American Consul General to Brazil.

115
00:07:11,720 --> 00:07:16,580
The Navy's own historians to this day list the cause as undetermined.

116
00:07:17,220 --> 00:07:21,500
Their leading guess and it is only a guess is that an overloaded,

117
00:07:21,780 --> 00:07:26,060
weakened hull broke apart in a storm recorded near the American coast around the same

118
00:07:26,060 --> 00:07:31,280
time. That is a guess not a conclusion because there is no wreck to confirm it.

119
00:07:32,660 --> 00:07:36,060
During the war some suspected a German raider or submarine.

120
00:07:36,560 --> 00:07:39,160
After the war German naval records were checked.

121
00:07:39,660 --> 00:07:42,880
There was no U -boat operating anywhere near that water on that date.

122
00:07:43,360 --> 00:07:46,880
The wartime rumor simply did not hold up once anyone actually looked.

123
00:07:48,320 --> 00:07:51,080
There is also a persistent rumor about the ship's own captain,

124
00:07:51,460 --> 00:07:53,220
a mutiny, a sabotage plot.

125
00:07:53,800 --> 00:07:57,700
No official investigation then or since has ever supported it.

126
00:07:57,900 --> 00:08:02,680
It survives purely as folklore layered onto a tragedy that already didn't need embellishing.

127
00:08:04,140 --> 00:08:07,240
Chapter four. The tanker that was never even in the triangle.

128
00:08:08,660 --> 00:08:13,820
1963. The tanker marine sulfur queen goes silent in the Florida Straits with 39

129
00:08:13,820 --> 00:08:16,720
crew aboard. No bodies. No survivors.

130
00:08:17,280 --> 00:08:19,380
A handful of debris found off the Florida Keys.

131
00:08:20,880 --> 00:08:24,480
The Coast Guard's own marine board of investigation did not call this a mystery.

132
00:08:24,900 --> 00:08:25,980
They called it a scandal.

133
00:08:26,840 --> 00:08:30,520
Fires in the tank area so routine the crew had stopped bothering to sound the alarm.

134
00:08:30,980 --> 00:08:34,220
A ruptured steam coil. Inoperable temperature gauges.

135
00:08:34,580 --> 00:08:37,820
A structural floor common to tankers converted the way this one was.

136
00:08:38,200 --> 00:08:41,460
Their verdict, the ship was unsafe and never should have sailed.

137
00:08:42,980 --> 00:08:45,660
The board considered four specific ways she could have gone down.

138
00:08:45,940 --> 00:08:48,100
A cargo explosion, a hull failure,

139
00:08:48,320 --> 00:08:49,900
a capsize from rolling in the swell,

140
00:08:50,180 --> 00:08:54,640
a steam explosion, and admitted that without survivors or wreckage they could not say

141
00:08:54,640 --> 00:08:59,700
which. That is a real sourced structural investigation with real findings.

142
00:09:00,060 --> 00:09:01,820
It is not a shrug into the unknown.

143
00:09:03,300 --> 00:09:06,040
And here is the detail that gets left out of every retelling.

144
00:09:06,600 --> 00:09:11,140
The marine sulfur queens sank in the Gulf of Mexico and the Florida Straits outside almost

145
00:09:11,140 --> 00:09:13,440
every version of the triangle's own boundary.

146
00:09:14,060 --> 00:09:18,480
A magazine writer branded her a triangle victim anyway two years after his own article

147
00:09:18,480 --> 00:09:20,700
ran. Nobody has corrected it since.

148
00:09:22,200 --> 00:09:25,920
Chapter five. The two British airliners Britain genuinely never got back.

149
00:09:27,340 --> 00:09:32,520
January 1948. A British airliner called Star Tiger vanishes on a flight from the Azores

150
00:09:32,520 --> 00:09:34,920
to Bermuda. 31 people are lost,

151
00:09:35,040 --> 00:09:37,960
including a decorated Royal Air Force air marshal from the war.

152
00:09:38,280 --> 00:09:39,520
No wreckage is ever found.

153
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:46,060
The official British court of inquiry did not blame whether mechanical failure or sabotage.

154
00:09:46,560 --> 00:09:51,600
It stated plainly that in the complete absence of reliable evidence the court

155
00:09:51,600 --> 00:09:53,860
could not do more than suggest possibilities,

156
00:09:54,340 --> 00:09:56,660
none of which reached even the level of probability.

157
00:09:57,280 --> 00:09:59,240
The fate of Star Tiger, they concluded,

158
00:09:59,660 --> 00:10:01,540
must remain an unsolved mystery.

159
00:10:03,180 --> 00:10:06,800
Thirteen months later, a sister aircraft of the exact same type,

160
00:10:07,340 --> 00:10:10,560
Star Ariel, vanishes on a flight from Bermuda to Jamaica.

161
00:10:11,360 --> 00:10:16,220
20 people lost. A search covering 55 ,000 square miles finds nothing.

162
00:10:17,460 --> 00:10:21,200
The investigators report is almost word for word the same conclusion.

163
00:10:21,740 --> 00:10:22,720
Through lack of evidence,

164
00:10:23,040 --> 00:10:24,860
due to no wreckage having been found,

165
00:10:25,080 --> 00:10:27,020
the cause of the accident is unknown.

166
00:10:27,600 --> 00:10:30,060
No mechanical defect, no weather problem,

167
00:10:30,420 --> 00:10:35,620
no sabotage, just an honest official unknown twice 13

168
00:10:35,620 --> 00:10:40,720
months apart. These two are genuinely honestly unsolved.

169
00:10:41,220 --> 00:10:45,020
That is worth sitting with for a second because it proves this episode is not trying

170
00:10:45,020 --> 00:10:48,120
to explain away every single case with a clean answer.

171
00:10:48,640 --> 00:10:50,980
Some real mysteries stay real mysteries.

172
00:10:51,340 --> 00:10:54,220
They just never required anything beyond ordinary aviation,

173
00:10:54,620 --> 00:10:58,340
in ordinary weather, going wrong in a way nobody was ever able to prove.

174
00:11:00,040 --> 00:11:04,240
Chapter 6. The most famous name on the list was never anywhere near this water.

175
00:11:05,740 --> 00:11:09,780
Almost every Bermuda Triangle book ever written includes the Mary Celeste,

176
00:11:10,120 --> 00:11:12,780
found drifting and abandoned in 1872.

177
00:11:13,360 --> 00:11:14,740
What almost none of them mention.

178
00:11:15,200 --> 00:11:16,580
She was found near the Azores,

179
00:11:16,960 --> 00:11:20,080
in the eastern Atlantic, thousands of miles from Bermuda,

180
00:11:20,420 --> 00:11:22,140
in essentially the opposite ocean.

181
00:11:23,300 --> 00:11:25,200
One likely reason for the mix -up.

182
00:11:25,660 --> 00:11:27,720
A different ship with a nearly identical name,

183
00:11:27,920 --> 00:11:31,720
the Mary Celeste, genuinely did wreck near Bermuda a few years earlier.

184
00:11:32,200 --> 00:11:33,940
Two ships, one letter apart,

185
00:11:34,420 --> 00:11:38,400
eight years apart, and one geography accidentally borrowed the other's mystery forever.

186
00:11:39,760 --> 00:11:43,460
The likely real explanation for the real Mary Celeste is almost mundane.

187
00:11:43,900 --> 00:11:47,540
She was carrying over 1700 barrels of raw industrial alcohol,

188
00:11:47,840 --> 00:11:49,700
a slow leak, a vapor scare,

189
00:11:49,700 --> 00:11:53,920
and a crew who panicked into a lifeboat that was then lost at sea in bad weather.

190
00:11:54,660 --> 00:11:56,380
Insurance fraud was investigated at the time.

191
00:11:56,680 --> 00:11:59,400
It was never proven. No fraud.

192
00:11:59,840 --> 00:12:01,780
No mutiny. No mystery required.

193
00:12:02,200 --> 00:12:04,780
Just a famous name, borrowed from the wrong ocean,

194
00:12:05,040 --> 00:12:07,340
and kept in the story because it was already famous,

195
00:12:07,600 --> 00:12:09,060
not because it belonged there.

196
00:12:10,480 --> 00:12:13,220
Chapter 7. The librarian who killed the mystery.

197
00:12:14,940 --> 00:12:18,080
1975. A reference librarian named Larry Kucher,

198
00:12:18,260 --> 00:12:20,080
working at Arizona State University,

199
00:12:20,280 --> 00:12:22,740
keeps getting the same research request from students,

200
00:12:23,040 --> 00:12:24,940
help me find sources on the Bermuda Triangle,

201
00:12:25,220 --> 00:12:27,020
so he starts pulling the sources himself.

202
00:12:28,420 --> 00:12:30,080
He writes directly to the Navy,

203
00:12:30,440 --> 00:12:32,100
the Coast Guard, Lloyd's of London,

204
00:12:32,200 --> 00:12:33,780
and the National Weather Service.

205
00:12:34,320 --> 00:12:36,880
He reads newspaper microfilm going back decades.

206
00:12:37,520 --> 00:12:40,020
He conducts around 92 personal interviews.

207
00:12:40,860 --> 00:12:42,040
Nobody had done this before.

208
00:12:42,580 --> 00:12:45,520
Every previous author had simply cited the last author.

209
00:12:47,020 --> 00:12:49,040
What he found, case after case,

210
00:12:49,340 --> 00:12:53,040
a documented storm active at the exact time and location of a loss,

211
00:12:53,360 --> 00:12:55,760
simply never mentioned in the popular retelling.

212
00:12:56,500 --> 00:12:58,480
A ship logged as vanished that had,

213
00:12:58,660 --> 00:13:01,860
in fact, turned up safely in port weeks later.

214
00:13:02,300 --> 00:13:04,840
A plane reported as having disappeared that had,

215
00:13:05,020 --> 00:13:06,520
in front of a crowd of witnesses,

216
00:13:07,080 --> 00:13:08,360
crashed on a beach in Florida.

217
00:13:09,720 --> 00:13:13,880
He even tracked down one of the most repeated ghost ship stories in every triangle book

218
00:13:13,880 --> 00:13:17,900
and could not find a single primary record that it had ever happened at all.

219
00:13:18,560 --> 00:13:20,820
Not misreported, not exaggerated,

220
00:13:21,540 --> 00:13:24,080
simply invented, and then endlessly recopied.

221
00:13:25,660 --> 00:13:28,280
His conclusion was never that nothing tragic happened out there.

222
00:13:28,820 --> 00:13:30,660
Ships sank, planes went down,

223
00:13:31,140 --> 00:13:36,240
real people died. His conclusion was that the mystery on top of those tragedies was manufactured,

224
00:13:36,680 --> 00:13:41,160
one author at a time, each one copying the last author's errors instead of checking the

225
00:13:41,160 --> 00:13:42,540
primary record for themselves.

226
00:13:44,260 --> 00:13:46,560
Chapter eight, what the actual numbers say.

227
00:13:48,020 --> 00:13:51,640
The National Ocean Service puts it about as plainly as a government agency ever puts

228
00:13:51,640 --> 00:13:56,320
anything. There is no evidence that mysterious disappearances happen more often here

229
00:13:56,320 --> 00:13:59,700
than in any other large, heavily traveled stretch of ocean.

230
00:14:00,200 --> 00:14:02,000
That is the actual, current,

231
00:14:02,300 --> 00:14:05,440
official position of the agency that studies this water for a living.

232
00:14:06,680 --> 00:14:11,220
The United States Board on Geographic Names does not even recognize the Bermuda Triangle

233
00:14:11,220 --> 00:14:12,540
as an official place name.

234
00:14:13,120 --> 00:14:14,860
As far as the federal government is concerned,

235
00:14:15,220 --> 00:14:17,980
on paper, it does not officially exist.

236
00:14:19,640 --> 00:14:24,140
Lloyds of London, the insurance market whose entire business depends on correctly pricing

237
00:14:24,140 --> 00:14:27,200
real risk, has never charged more to cross this water.

238
00:14:27,680 --> 00:14:29,860
If there were an actual elevated hazard here,

239
00:14:30,120 --> 00:14:34,540
the people whose entire job is finding hazards and charging accordingly would have found

240
00:14:34,540 --> 00:14:36,260
it and priced it in decades ago.

241
00:14:37,480 --> 00:14:42,780
In 2013, the World Wildlife Fund ranked the world's oceans using real shipping

242
00:14:42,780 --> 00:14:44,640
loss data, going back over a decade.

243
00:14:45,340 --> 00:14:49,640
The Bermuda Triangle did not crack the top 10 most dangerous stretches of water on the

244
00:14:49,640 --> 00:14:54,680
entire planet. It landed around 12th by total losses well behind the South China

245
00:14:54,680 --> 00:14:55,800
Sea and the Mediterranean,

246
00:14:56,320 --> 00:14:59,880
and even further down the list once you account for how much traffic actually passes

247
00:14:59,880 --> 00:15:04,800
through. And the reason the raw body count still looks large is simple.

248
00:15:04,800 --> 00:15:08,020
This is one of the busiest patches of ocean on the planet.

249
00:15:08,640 --> 00:15:13,100
Miami and Port Canaveral alone move something like 17 million cruise passengers a year.

250
00:15:13,820 --> 00:15:16,880
San Juan's airport boards roughly 12 ,000 people a day.

251
00:15:17,360 --> 00:15:18,860
More traffic, mechanically,

252
00:15:19,440 --> 00:15:23,420
produces more incidents, even when the underlying rate is completely ordinary.

253
00:15:24,780 --> 00:15:28,740
The Gulf Stream itself explains a lot of the rest in two separate ways.

254
00:15:29,180 --> 00:15:32,020
First, it is a fast, powerful current,

255
00:15:32,280 --> 00:15:34,160
and where it collides with different air masses,

256
00:15:34,160 --> 00:15:36,100
it breeds sudden, violent weather.

257
00:15:36,860 --> 00:15:41,140
Second, it is extremely good at scattering wreckage and debris before anyone can reach

258
00:15:41,140 --> 00:15:43,820
it. That is a mundane, physical,

259
00:15:44,380 --> 00:15:45,900
boring reason evidence goes missing.

260
00:15:46,340 --> 00:15:48,100
It is not evidence of anything else.

261
00:15:49,440 --> 00:15:53,300
An Australian scientist who has studied this put the human error math simply,

262
00:15:53,720 --> 00:15:58,000
the percentage of ships and planes that go missing here is the same as anywhere else

263
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:02,980
in the world. It is ordinary probability applied to an extraordinary amount of traffic.

264
00:16:04,480 --> 00:16:06,620
And just south of it lies the Puerto Rico Trench,

265
00:16:07,040 --> 00:16:08,400
plunging to the Milwaukee Deep,

266
00:16:08,780 --> 00:16:11,660
the single deepest point in the entire Atlantic Ocean.

267
00:16:12,540 --> 00:16:16,460
Sometimes a wreck is never found for the single most boring reason there is.

268
00:16:16,820 --> 00:16:18,960
It is sitting five miles down.

269
00:16:20,320 --> 00:16:24,840
Chapter 9, the theories that never held up and the ones still running right now.

270
00:16:25,880 --> 00:16:29,220
You will hear that pockets of methane trapped under the seafloor could erupt without

271
00:16:29,220 --> 00:16:32,840
warning and sink a ship or ignite into a fireball in the air above it.

272
00:16:33,280 --> 00:16:37,460
A government geologist who studies exactly this kind of seafloor gas has said there is

273
00:16:37,460 --> 00:16:41,680
no evidence of a release anything like that happening in this region in the last 15 ,000

274
00:16:41,680 --> 00:16:45,820
years. You will hear that compasses go strange here,

275
00:16:45,820 --> 00:16:50,920
that this is one of only two places on Earth where true north and magnetic north perfectly

276
00:16:50,920 --> 00:16:53,280
align. That effect is real.

277
00:16:53,700 --> 00:16:58,800
It is also basic navigational science taught to every sailor and pilot who has ever earned

278
00:16:58,800 --> 00:17:02,980
a license and it exists at multiple longitudes scattered around the planet,

279
00:17:03,240 --> 00:17:04,920
drifting slowly over time,

280
00:17:05,200 --> 00:17:07,320
not fixed uniquely to this one triangle.

281
00:17:08,500 --> 00:17:12,820
A real oceanographer at the University of Southampton went further than most and actually

282
00:17:12,820 --> 00:17:14,240
tested a theory in a lab.

283
00:17:14,760 --> 00:17:18,800
He proposed that storms converging from three directions at once could generate rogue

284
00:17:18,800 --> 00:17:24,200
waves up to 100 feet tall and he built a scale model of a hull shaped like the USS Cyclops

285
00:17:24,200 --> 00:17:26,840
and put it through a wave tank to see how it would behave.

286
00:17:28,280 --> 00:17:33,420
The model showed real vulnerability to a wave snapping along overloaded hull like hers.

287
00:17:33,880 --> 00:17:36,440
That is genuine, serious oceanography.

288
00:17:36,980 --> 00:17:39,900
What it is not is unique to this stretch of water.

289
00:17:40,600 --> 00:17:44,680
Storm convergence rogue waves are a documented hazard anywhere three weather systems

290
00:17:44,680 --> 00:17:48,140
can meet at sea, not a phenomenon invented for this one triangle.

291
00:17:49,520 --> 00:17:54,560
In 2014 a television programme showed satellite photos of strange hexagon

292
00:17:54,560 --> 00:17:58,800
shaped clouds over the region and presented them as a newly solved piece of the mystery.

293
00:17:59,400 --> 00:18:04,240
Microbursts the show called air bombs sudden violent downdrafts strong enough to sink

294
00:18:04,240 --> 00:18:09,360
a ship or down a plane. The meteorologist who provided the original explanation

295
00:18:09,360 --> 00:18:12,240
later told a reporter that what actually aired was,

296
00:18:12,560 --> 00:18:15,400
in his own words, an editing job that was horrendous.

297
00:18:16,080 --> 00:18:19,600
He said he had offered the network an illustrative straw man example,

298
00:18:19,980 --> 00:18:23,000
never a solved mystery. They sold it as an answer anyway.

299
00:18:24,480 --> 00:18:28,700
These hexagonal cloud formations turn up at plenty of other ocean locations worldwide.

300
00:18:29,300 --> 00:18:33,380
That is the entire mechanism on camera admitted by the scientist involved.

301
00:18:33,900 --> 00:18:38,400
A real detail stretched past what it can support for a better hour of television.

302
00:18:39,840 --> 00:18:42,780
And the machine is still running today in real time.

303
00:18:43,260 --> 00:18:48,180
A claim now circulates online about a sonar survey that supposedly found a perfectly

304
00:18:48,180 --> 00:18:51,440
glassy geometric triangle carved into the seafloor.

305
00:18:51,940 --> 00:18:53,540
No university has published it.

306
00:18:53,920 --> 00:18:55,860
No named scientist stands behind it.

307
00:18:56,220 --> 00:18:58,200
No actual study has ever been located.

308
00:18:58,760 --> 00:19:01,440
It has every hallmark of being manufactured content,

309
00:19:01,440 --> 00:19:06,840
not a real finding. The exact same pattern Vincent Gaddis started in 1964 is

310
00:19:06,840 --> 00:19:10,820
still generating brand new fake evidence right now.

311
00:19:11,040 --> 00:19:14,400
Chapter 10. Why we wanted it to be true in the first place.

312
00:19:15,900 --> 00:19:17,680
None of this required anyone to be lying.

313
00:19:18,500 --> 00:19:21,440
Exactly. A magazine writer needed a good cover story.

314
00:19:22,060 --> 00:19:25,800
A television producer needed a satisfying ending before the credits rolled.

315
00:19:26,640 --> 00:19:29,240
Every single link in that chain made sense on its own.

316
00:19:30,080 --> 00:19:32,120
The mystery was never built by one person.

317
00:19:32,820 --> 00:19:34,420
It was built by dozens of people,

318
00:19:34,700 --> 00:19:36,660
each one making the story slightly better,

319
00:19:37,040 --> 00:19:42,440
one retelling at a time. A documented storm is not a satisfying answer.

320
00:19:42,900 --> 00:19:47,280
A structural flaw in a converted oil tanker is not a satisfying answer.

321
00:19:47,780 --> 00:19:49,800
An honest, official, unknown,

322
00:19:50,240 --> 00:19:54,220
twice, 13 months apart is not a satisfying answer.

323
00:19:54,720 --> 00:19:57,340
A cursed triangle that swallows ships whole is.

324
00:19:57,700 --> 00:20:00,180
The unsatisfying answer usually wins the argument.

325
00:20:00,580 --> 00:20:01,820
It rarely wins the retelling.

326
00:20:03,180 --> 00:20:06,320
That is not a flaw unique to this one triangle of ocean.

327
00:20:06,680 --> 00:20:11,160
It's the same mechanism behind almost every manufactured mystery you'll ever be shown.

328
00:20:11,560 --> 00:20:13,200
An editor who wants a better ending,

329
00:20:13,560 --> 00:20:17,220
an author who trusts the last author's version instead of the primary record,

330
00:20:17,480 --> 00:20:21,260
and an audience that would honestly rather believe in something strange than sit with

331
00:20:21,260 --> 00:20:25,220
something merely tragic. Here's what stays with me.

332
00:20:25,700 --> 00:20:30,080
Every single one of these cases that actually received a real investigation ended in

